    "interpretation": "The operationally important number is the collision rate: 0.267% of the sampled OQMD structures (72/27000, 67 distinct fingerprints out of 27,000 checked) happen to carry a fingerprint identical to one in the marked MP-20 collection produced under the MP-20-specific key. This is expected and benign at the level of the FULL OQMD pool -- auditing the entire 27,000-structure OQMD sample under the MP-20 key does not fire (p=0.244, green_fraction=0.502, matching the null gamma=0.5 exactly) -- because the ~72 colliding structures are diluted by 26,928 non-colliding ones. But an auditor who mistakenly believed a SMALL, already-suspicious OQMD subset (e.g. one they pulled because it looked unusual, mirroring the ~72-structure collision set here) came from the marked generator would find that subset audits as 100% green and rejects at p=6.8e-21 -- a false implication driven entirely by coincidental fingerprint collision, not generation. The mixed-collection test (marked MP-20 diluted 1:1 with random OQMD) still rejects (green_fraction=0.744, p=0), showing the audit is robust to substantial contamination by an unrelated database as long as the true marked fraction is not diluted below the auditability frontier. The risk is asymmetric: a SMALL, cherry-picked cross-database subset can trigger a false positive at high confidence; a LARGE, representative cross-database sample will not."
  },
